<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Iloilo City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Iloilo City is a port city on the south-east coast of Panay in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Opened to foreign trade in 1855, it prospered in the 19th century as the outlet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> from Panay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negros">Negros</a> and for local textiles, and its merchant families led the revolution in the region in 1898. The Spanish garrison surrendered there in December 1898, and American forces took the city in February 1899.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/iloilo-city.jpg" alt="Iloilo City" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrickroque01 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iloilo_River,_Tabucan,_Mandurriao_drone_(Iloilo_City;_01-24-2026).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Iloilo City lies on the southern shore of Panay, facing the Iloilo Strait and the island of Guimaras, which together form a natural harbour and a safe anchorage for ships. The Iloilo and Batiano rivers flow through its districts into the strait. The name derives from the older Irong-irong, meaning nose-like, which referred to the promontory between the two rivers where Fort San Pedro and the 17th-century Spanish port stood. The city is a conglomeration of former towns, now seven districts: the City Proper, Jaro, Molo, Mandurriao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a>, Arevalo and Lapuz. In 1930 the architect Juan Arellano drew a plan for it based on Ebenezer Howard's Garden City concept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest written reference to Iloilo appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> records of the 1300s, which identify Oton, west of the city, as a bustling trade outpost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visayas">Visayas</a>. In 1565 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> sent his grandson Felipe de Salcedo and Augustinian missionaries, among them Martín de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radaa">Rada</a>, to explore the Visayas for resources, and they set up a temporary settlement at Araut on Panay to secure food supplies. In 1566 the Spanish settled in the area, which became the second Spanish colonial centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>. Mexican soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> reinforced the garrison in 1603, 1636, 1670 and 1672.</p><p>Queen Regent Maria Christina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> gave the city the title La Muy Leal y Noble Ciudad, Most Loyal and Noble City, in recognition of its loyalty to the Spanish crown during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a>. Iloilo served as the last capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> in Asia and the Pacific before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1898. By 1900 it was widely known as the Queen City of the South, and it was considered the second most important city in the Philippines after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>. Japanese forces invaded Panay in 1942. A fire in 1966 destroyed nearly three quarters of the downtown area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Iloilo City is the regional centre of Western Visayas and the capital of Iloilo Province, though it is administered independently of the province. It had 473,728 inhabitants in the 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and Hiligaynon is the predominant language. The Calle Real Heritage Zone preserves buildings of the Spanish and American periods. The city is the centre of devotion to Nuestra Señora de la Candelaria de Jaro, and holds the Dinagyang Festival every January. UNESCO recognises it as a Creative City of Gastronomy, known for dishes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-paz">La Paz</a> batchoy and pancit Molo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Iloilo City” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As the revolution spread to the Visayan islands in 1898, local leaders in Panay organised a cantonal government in <b>Iloilo</b>, inspired by federalist ideas.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-the-visayas">Federal State of the Visayas</a></li></ul></section>
